## 2. UNIFORM-MARGIN INSPECTION — the uniform ε* (§3) is stronger than needed and is the harder claim

LB-ARCH certifies ε₀ ≈ 0.008 at ONE station with an explicit, auditable basin-continuity margin (spectral gap
0.785; unstable-direction rotation bound ‖h‖/0.785; superlevel capture clearance 0.986; det-margin 0.0196;
no-new-critical margin 0.00802). GP-DER-112 §3 instead asserts a SINGLE ε* holding UNIFORMLY over a
positive-width box B', inheriting it wholesale from GP-DER-106.

Observation (recommended simplification): the conclusion does NOT require a uniform ε*. The disintegration §7
needs only, for each u∈B', SOME positive robustness radius ε(u)>0 with N_u ⊆ A_{r*} and Q_u(N_u)>0; a
measurable (e.g. lower-semicontinuous) map u ↦ ε(u) already makes
E* = {f : J(f)∈B', ‖S_{r*}f − P_{J(f)}^(4)‖_{C²(D⁺)} < ε(J(f))} Borel, and §7's argument ("a measurable
positive function on a positive-measure set has positive integral") goes through verbatim. GP-DER-112 §7 itself
already renounces a uniform LOWER BOUND on Q_u(N_u); by the same logic it can renounce the uniform ε*.
RECOMMENDATION: downgrade §3's "there exists ε*>0 … for every u in B'" to "there exists a measurable
ε: B'→(0,∞)…". This removes the single hardest-to-certify uniformity claim and isolates the true dependency
(§3 below). It is a strict weakening of the hypotheses with no loss to the theorem.

## 3. WHERE THE LOAD ACTUALLY SITS — GP-DER-106 capture-OPENNESS, not capture-by-integration

The one genuinely non-standard, still-GP-side ingredient is GP-DER-106's certification that the selected-branch
CAPTURE / convergence-to-M property (§3 items 3–6) is an OPEN condition in the C² topology with a positive
robustness radius. LB-ARCH shows exactly what such a certificate must contain to be valid: a λ-lemma /
structural-stability margin (unstable-direction rotation bounded by ‖h‖/spectral-gap), a superlevel-capture
clearance, and completeness of the critical-point census so that "no saddle exists to reroute the branch."
Capture demonstrated by merely INTEGRATING a trajectory to M (as opposed to a trapping-region + strict-margin
argument) is NOT a C²-open property and would not transfer to the conditional-support neighborhood. So the
decisive question for AO48/CW is narrow and concrete: does GP-DER-106 certify capture via strict trapping /
λ-lemma margins (per-u positive, and measurable in u over B'), or only by trajectory integration? If the
former, §3 holds (in the weakened per-u form of §2) and the fixed-r theorem stands; if the latter, GP-DER-112
needs amendment. This is the single item I recommend AO48/CW focus on.

## 4. SHARPEST STRUCTURAL DIFFERENCE — the interior window-saddle is NOT frozen

LB-ARCH pins NINE functionals: value+gradient at M, S, AND the interior arch saddle y* — so y* is an exact
frozen equilibrium of every field in the ensemble, and its "no-new-criticals / routing" control borrows that
rigidity directly. GP-DER-112 pins only SIX functionals (value+gradient at M and S) and additionally CONDITIONS
on nine higher-order midpoint JETS; it does NOT freeze any interior critical point. Consequence: GP-DER-112's
control of interior typing, extra criticals, and branch routing CANNOT borrow interior-pin rigidity the way
LB-ARCH does — it must come ENTIRELY from GP-DER-106's degree-four trapping margins on the scaled model P_u^(4).
This is not an error, but it is precisely why the whole weight of §3 rests on GP-DER-106 and why the capture
must be a genuine open-condition (trapping/λ-lemma) certificate rather than an inherited pin rigidity. It is the
sharpest point of the assigned comparison and the place a skeptical AO48/CW reviewer should press hardest.

